     "result_text_tail": "-making promotions, reinstating receiving and rotation checks, and refusing the delivery provider's 3% rise. That lifts run-rate operating income to about $23M, above the lender's $20M minimum.\n- **Decisions requested:** four, on the closing slide.\n\n**Choices I made where I would have asked you** (also listed on the last appendix slide)\n- **Fiscal years:** I read FY2025 as February 2025 to January 2026. That means FY2026 closes just before the board meeting, so the covenant year's result is largely already set. The deck tests the covenant on the run-rate after the first actions and recommends briefing the lender early.\n- **Vendor funding:** I read the 14-point promotion margin gap as being after vendor funding. That implies about $113M of markdowns. If the pack meant it before funding, this lever needs re-sizing.\n- **The nine weak stores:** the pack gives only totals, so I split the six and the three in proportion to store count.\n- **Costs not in the pack:** apart from the $7.5M lease-exit cost, all one-time costs are my planning estimates and are labelled as such.\n- **Wage rise:** I applied the 4% minimum-wage increase to the whole store pay bill, which is the cautious choice. If it hits only half the pay scale, the plan reaches about 3.2%.\n- **Promotions:** I assumed 30% of the margin recovered from cutting promotions is lost again through lower volume.\n\nFiles are in `./deliverable/`:\n- `Harlow_Margin_Plan_Board_Feb2027.pptx`\n- `Harlow_Margin_Plan_Board_Feb2027_preview.pdf`"
    }
